// SOS M3 unit 3: A SIBLING WITHOUT `Mint` CANNOT MINT (sawos design 4, as ruled
// Aug 29).
//
// **THE PROPERTY THAT MAKES MINTING SAFE TO GIVE AWAY.** `MINT_OP` is gated on
// the universal `Mint` bit, and `Mint` is a bit like any other in the keep mask
// — so a launcher may hand out a handle that can be used and cannot be
// multiplied. Without that, one `Mint` anywhere would be `Mint` everywhere
// downstream forever, and attenuating a chain would mean never starting one.
//
//   SOS mintrevoked: minted without Mint
//   SOS: process fault: access denied process=0
//   SOS: process teardown ... process=0
//
// **IT IS ALSO THE ONLY WAY TO HOLD A SOURCE THAT LACKS `Mint` AT ALL**, which
// is why one transcript carries two claims that read as separate rulings. Every
// default set in the system grants `Mint` (the uniform lean), so a handle
// without it is necessarily one somebody MASKED — and the refusal proved here is
// simultaneously "the sibling cannot mint" and "a mint through a source lacking
// `Mint` is `AccessDenied`". A second case would have been the same program.
//
// THE SOURCE IS ROOT'S OWN SYSTEM HANDLE and the first mint SUCCEEDS, which is
// what makes the second refusal mean something: the op works, the caller holds a
// perfectly good handle onto the machine's System object, and the ONE thing it
// cannot do is the thing its mask took away. A kernel that ignored the bit would
// print the survival line instead.
//
// The mask is spelled as a COMPLEMENT — everything except `Mint` — which is the
// notation the polarity ruling leaves available for exactly this: the case means
// "the same authority, minus the power to copy it".

import sos.{System, SosStatus, SystemRight}

static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

/// Everything this handle has, EXCEPT the universal mint bit. The
/// `& 0xFFFF_FFFF` keeps the fold inside `UInt32`: Saw folds constants in the
/// signed platform-`Int` domain, where a bare `~x` is negative.
static NO_MINT: UInt32 = ~(SystemRight.Mint as UInt32) & 0xFFFF_FFFF

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    // ONE: a sibling of root's System handle, carrying everything root has
    // except the authority to make another. The op is ungated by anything but
    // `Mint`, which root holds, so this succeeds.
    let narrowed = try system.mint(rights: NO_MINT) catch {
        print("SOS mintrevoked: first mint failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS mintrevoked: minted without Mint\n")

    // TWO: ask the sibling for a sibling. The chain stops here — this does not
    // return, because a source without `Mint` is a caller error and design 178's
    // faults ruling ends the process that made it.
    let _ = narrowed.mint()

    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS mintrevoked: survived, which is the bug\n")
    let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
}
